ng-average history, so raw CRPS is the decision statistic until 10-day competition runs.\n+- **Verdict:** Keep.\n+- **Lesson:** Fast variance adaptation captures a large late-regime gain without sacrificing the quieter early window. The next bottleneck is distribution shape and asset heterogeneity, not basic volatility responsiveness.\n+\n+## Iteration 3 — Cross-asset calibration map\n+\n+- **Hypothesis:** A single pooled volatility blend is directionally sound but leaves systematic asset-specific dispersion errors, especially for high-coefficient SP500, AAPLX, XAU, GOOGLX, TSLAX, and NVDAX. Mapping official CRPS across assets will identify whether scale calibration should precede tail modeling.\n+- **Change:** No simulator change. Replace the evaluator's strict loader with a partition-wise lenient loader that preserves NaNs, then measure one-day 200-path official backtests across all active assets on early- and late-August windows.\n+- **Result:** Pending.\n+- **Verdict:** Pending.\n+- **Lesson:** Pending.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\ngit add agent/predict.py agent/journal.md && git commit -m \\\"support sparse snapshot evaluation\\\"\nuv run agent/predict.py --asset XAU --days 1 --eval-end 2026-08-15 --num-simulations 20 | tail -5\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
